WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.800
A container threat model starts with the application, but it does not end there.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:09.220
Application code, base images, dependencies, build systems, registries,

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.720
runtime configuration, host operating systems, runtimes, orchestrators, cloud platforms,

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.900
identities, service accounts, secrets,

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.160
and environment variables all contribute to the attack surface.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:26.260
Each layer can introduce risk, and each layer can also provide useful control points.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.800
Container isolation is useful,

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.900
but it should not be treated as a complete security boundary.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:34.280
Containers commonly share the host kernel,

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:39.220
may have network reachability to internal services, may mount volumes,

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.480
and may run with identities that can access other systems.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:48.220
A hardened configuration can reduce risk, while a privileged container, broad host mount,

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:53.080
weak secret handling, or excessive service account permission can greatly increase impact.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.500
Shared responsibility is practical, not just contractual.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:02.160
Developers influence application code, dependency choices, Dockerfiles, build context,

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.460
and whether secrets are accidentally included.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.400
Platform teams influence runtime defaults, orchestration policy, namespaces,

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.340
network controls, and deployment patterns.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:17.120
Security teams help define policy, review risk, monitor behavior,

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.500
and respond to incidents.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.920
Operations teams keep infrastructure healthy and recoverable.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.460
Cloud providers or hosting teams secure the underlying services they operate.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.880
Good container security programs make responsibility explicit.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.580
Someone must own image provenance, registry access, pipeline approvals,

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:41.160
runtime configuration, patch and rebuild workflows, secrets rotation, logging,

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.060
and incident response.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.200
When ownership is vague, security gaps often appear between teams.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.260
The container may run successfully,

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.920
but nobody is accountable for whether it was built from trusted inputs,

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:56.340
deployed with least privilege, or monitored after release.
